The find_assertions function checks each line for a comment that starts with "@@@". It then splits the comment up into each word, and checks if the word is an 'OLD()' or 'RET'. If it is, it extracts out the specific variable or operation. The function then constructs an assert statement with the appropriate variables and operations. If the assert statement needs to access the value of a variables at the beginning of the function, it can use the 'OLD(variable_name)' syntax. 

Note that in C#, it's not possible to extract an old value for a local variable or return value of a method during runtime due to the way C# compiles the code to IL (Intermediate Language). 

To extract the variable values at runtime for an assertion you would typically compile the code into an assembly, which the Debugger can inspect during runtime. This isn't trivial though, since most C# code is run within a managed runtime environment by the CLR, not a standalone program.--------------------Here is an answer for the code:

```cs
﻿using System;
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList
{
    // @@@ Assert that the capacity is never incremented beyond MaxArrayLength
    Debug.Assert(Capacity <= MaxArrayLength);

    // @@@ Capacity is always greater than or equal to size
    Debug.Assert(Capacity >= Count);

    // @@@ The index of a particular item in the ArrayList is always within the range of the ArrayList
    Debug.Assert(IndexOf(obj) >= 0 && IndexOf(obj) < Count);

    // @@@ If the list contains x, last index of x must be less than array size
    Debug.Assert(LastIndexOf(obj) < Count);
}
```
This code defines some assertions for a class named "ArrayList". We assume that all the assertions are made in a testing context after some operations have been performed.

These assertions include:
1. "Assert that the capacity is never incremented beyond MaxArrayLength." This asserts the ArrayList's capacity will not exceed MaxValue for integer.
2. "Capacity is always greater than or equal to size." This asserts the ArrayList's capacity is always greater than or equal to the count of items in the list.
3. "The index of a particular item in the ArrayList is always within the range of the ArrayList." This asserts any index used in the ArrayList is always between 0 and the count - 1.
4. "If the list contains x, last index of x must be less than array size." This asserts that if 'x' is contained in the list, the last index of 'x' will be less than the count.

